WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] This is the
first tweet ever posted

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.320
by Twitter's co-founder, Jack Dorsey.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.820
And this is a tweet by Elon Musk,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.240
the richest man in the world,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.950
announcing a $43 billion bid

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.780
to take over the platform 16 years later.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.030
His offer sparked national coverage.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.720
- Billionaire Elon Musk,
he's offered to buy Twitter.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.340
- For $54.20 cents a share.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.350
- And says he now wants to turn

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.850
Twitter into the world's
"platform for free speech."

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.250
- [Narrator] Since that
first tweet in 2006,

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.290
several different contenders
have looked to acquire Twitter.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.330
Let's look back at
Twitter's previous suitors

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.570
to figure out why those
ultimately fell through

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:41.280
and what could be
different about this one.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.400
Central to understanding
Twitter's battle for leadership

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.030
is co-founder Jack Dorsey.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.240
- Jack Dorsey comes with this
generation of entrepreneurs

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.670
in the Valley who really saw
a new way of communicating.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.250
But Twitter has been a company

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.420
that has long had its ups and downs.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.300
- [Narrator] Dorsey's first
run as CEO ended after one year

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.090
amid concerns that he was
distracted by other hobbies.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.260
But in 2015, after he had
founded another company,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.740
and as Twitter looked to
revive its sagging user growth,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.910
Dorsey was reappointed to the position.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.510
The hope was that his founder title

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.410
would afford him the leeway to
make more significant changes

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.250
than Twitter's previous CEO.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.320
- It was seen as a huge comeback.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.070
There was comparisons to when
Steve Jobs returned to Apple.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.420
- [Narrator] Just one year after
Dorsey reclaimed the reins,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.010
and nearly three since
the company's flashy IPO,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.320
some of the biggest names
in tech and entertainment

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.480
looked to buy Twitter.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.450
- Jack Dorsey has said

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:36.740
essentially Twitter is always for sale,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.400
has always been for sale.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.850
The issue around Twitter is that it's,

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.560
there's often seen as this potential prize

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.850
that big tech could buy.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.410
And that's kind of what
Jack Dorsey's getting at,

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.180
is that there's this thing out here

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.770
that people think that
if they gussy it up,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.520
it could have incredible value.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.300
- [Narrator] In September 2016,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.240
CNBC reported that the
software company Salesforce

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.170
was considering a takeover
of the social media company.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.640
Salesforce already had a
partnership with Twitter,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.180
which began in 2012.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.030
That alliance allowed Twitter
to feed social media data

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.520
into Salesforce's analytic systems.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.550
- At the time, the interest
in Twitter was simple.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:15.630
It was about the data.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.010
Salesforce thought there
was useful information

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.120
to be gleaned

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.930
and that it could have value
to its corporate customers.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:24.970
- [Narrator] At the same time,

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.680
the Walt Disney Company
considered an offer of its own.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.540
In this case, it was Twitter's
moves into sports streaming,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.010
rather than a focus on data,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.198
that attracted the attention
of then-CEO, Robert Iger.

00:02:35.198 --> 00:02:38.590
- ESPN, a sports property
within the Disney family,

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:39.770
was exploring those efforts,

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:41.870
and they thought there
might be a potential

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.410
that Twitter could beef up that offering.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.550
- [Narrator] And then, there
was Google's parent, Alphabet.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.840
For several years, the
tech company's dominance

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.420
in search and advertising
caused some analysts

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.240
to speculate that it would be
best suited to buy Twitter.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.130
But the company choosing to
focus on its other ventures,

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.890
like driverless cars and
artificial intelligence,

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.210
ultimately didn't follow
through with an offer.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.170
Salesforce was the final
suitor remaining in 2016,

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.530
after others like Disney

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.520
grew skittish over Twitter's image.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.040
- Disney has a lot of brands within it,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.520
but at the core of that company
is family entertainment.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.530
And one of the things that you
increasingly see on Twitter

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.800
is a lot of unfamily-like activities.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.020
It's easy to understand why a company

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.950
most associated with Mickey Mouse

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.750
might not want to get into the gutter.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.640
- [Narrator] Ultimately,
Salesforce walked away

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.410
from pursuing a combination too.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:34.180
- One of the challenges for any company

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.550
thinking about buying Twitter

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.690
is you are stepping into a tech company,

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.310
but you're also stepping into a company

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.640
that has enormous policy
decisions on its plate right now

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.140
regarding freedom of speech,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.810
regarding content moderation.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.900
- [Narrator] The policy
concerns of buyers like Disney

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.620
are now the reason for Musk's
desire to acquire Twitter.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.030
- Elon Musk has framed
his interest in Twitter

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.490
simply as fighting to protect
the freedom of speech.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.150
Elon seems to be motivated
by his civic concerns.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:10.160
Now, we cannot kind of divorce
the reality of the situation

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.070
and that is Elon Musk
would benefit perhaps.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.840
- [Narrator] In a Ted Talk on April 14th,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.430
Musk provided further details

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.350
about his plan to take Twitter private.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.070
- I should also say that the intent

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.470
is to retain as many shareholders

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.940
as is allowed by the law

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.310
in a private company, which I
think is around 2,000 or so.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.390
- Elon Musk's comments

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.483
about wanting to keep
shareholders involved in Twitter

00:04:33.483 --> 00:04:35.290
raised a lot of red flags

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.670
with people who have watched
him operate in the past.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.040
It's something similar he said

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.190
when he wanted to take Tesla private.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.380
And there are rules
about how many investors

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.290
can be involved in
these private companies.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.300
And that is one of the things

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.560
that when he killed that
deal to take Tesla private

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.780
was pointed at and is
part of the rationale

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.166
for why it didn't work.

00:04:54.166 --> 00:04:56.030
- [Narrator] But if his offer is accepted,

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.500
he won't be the first billionaire

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.540
to own a key player in the media.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:03.160
- There is a long history
of people with money

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.900
looking for trophy purchases

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.930
that give them power and influence.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.050
And for Elon Musk, owning Twitter,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.020
controlling that pipeline

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.100
into millions and
millions of people's lives

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.100
around the world,

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.090
that would be incredibly powerful

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.700
and it would give him some assurance

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.860
that he could continue to use that tool.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.390
- [Narrator] Musk may
not be the only suitor

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:27.280
for the company.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.050
Some private equity firms
are now circling as well,

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.550
but it's still too early to know

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.400
if any will make a formal bid.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.240
In the meantime,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.950
Twitter has adopted a poison pill

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.180
that makes it hard for
shareholders to build their stake

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.694
beyond a set point in
response to Musk's bid.

00:05:40.694 --> 00:05:43.361
(upbeat music)

